# Force LF line endings for files where it actually matters.
#
# Why this exists: Windows git defaults to `core.autocrlf=true` which
# rewrites every text file's LF → CRLF at checkout. For PowerShell
# scripts that contain `@"..."@` here-strings, the CRLF terminator
# can trip Windows PowerShell 5.1's stricter parser and produce a
# bogus "The string is missing the terminator" error. Pinning .ps1
# (and the obviously-LF assets below) to LF avoids the conversion.

# Scripts the cross-platform installer runs
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.ps1  text eol=lf

# Windows batch files genuinely want CRLF (some Windows shells choke
# on LF-only .cmd files), so let git default-detect on those.

# Lock files + JSON are LF everywhere
*.json     text eol=lf
package-lock.json text eol=lf
